        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "44 is the number of cars.",
          "worked": "Add the frequencies to count cars:\n$$7+21+11+5=44\\text{ cars}.$$\nEach car may carry more than one passenger, so 44 is not the total number of passengers.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 12
      },
      {
        "id": "p6-q7-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p6-q7-b-p",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Work out the total number of passengers in the cars.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "102 passengers. Multiply each passenger count by its frequency, then add the products.",
          "worked": "Multiply each passenger count by the number of cars with that count, then add.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Total passengers}&=1\\times7+2\\times21+3\\times11+4\\times5\\\\\\text{Total passengers}&=7+42+33+20\\\\\\text{Total passengers}&=102\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      }
    ]
